course: dictionary
lesson: 8301
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Rivet is a noun and a verb, and it means different things in each job. The context, meaning the words around it, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «Metal rivets on jeans were patented in 1873 to stop pockets tearing.» Jeans and pockets are the clues: a rivet here is a short metal pin.
explain: «The film riveted us from the first scene.» Riveted does not mean fastened with pins here. The clue is the film: it held our attention completely, so it must mean we could not look away. Ask yourself what word would fit, then swap your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: Some phrases are fixed, so learn them as a whole. "Riveted to the spot" means unable to move, from fear or surprise. A character who is riveted to the spot is frozen, not stuck to the floor with pins. Hearing the phrase in a story and seeing what happens next is the best way to remember it.
fact: A rivet is a short metal pin; its end is hammered flat to hold it in place.
fact: To rivet means to fasten something with rivets, or to hold someone's attention completely.
fact: «A riveting story» is so exciting you cannot stop reading.
fact: «Riveted to the spot» means unable to move, from fear or surprise.
fact: Ships, bridges and planes have thousands of rivets, which join their metal sheets together.
exercise: «Metal rivets on jeans were patented in 1873 to stop pockets tearing.» What is a rivet here? | A kind of pocket | A type of trouser | A small bird | *A short metal pin used to join pieces of metal or cloth
exercise: «The film riveted us from the first scene.» What does riveted mean? | Hammered flat | Frozen in fear | Fastened with metal pins | *Held our attention completely
exercise: Which phrase means unable to move, from fear or surprise? | Metal rivets on a ship | Riveted to the screen | *Riveted to the spot | A riveting book
test: «Ships, bridges and planes have thousands of rivets.» Is rivets a noun or a verb here? | Adjective | Adverb | Verb | *Noun
test: «The story was so riveting I forgot my lunch.» What does riveting mean? | Made of metal | *So exciting you cannot stop reading or watching | Very cold | Dull and slow
exam: Which is the best way to work out a word with more than one meaning? | Skip the word and never come back | Use a dictionary only and never read the sentence | *Read the clues in the sentence, then swap in your meaning and check it | Guess the first meaning you think of
